WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.840
Adding cables to a knit,

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.600
maybe just the designer touch you need.
Just like any other stitch pattern, cables

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.560
impact the gauge of your knitting and gauge
can only be determined by swatching.

4
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.640
Now, before you leave me.
Hang in there.

5
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.360
I do have a shortcut.
Before we get to the shortcut.

6
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.000
If you are planning an all-over cable

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.160
sweater, for example, just like any
other stitch pattern you have to swatch.

8
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.880
Think about it. As you're crossing
the cables, you're not only impacting

9
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.160
the width of your knitting,
but the length as well.

10
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.840
Cables are going to shorten the knitting.

11
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.800
The difference in rows is really obvious
in this swatch.

12
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.200
Can you see how much shorter that center
cable section is in this small swatch?

13
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.760
Imagine the difference
over an entire sweater.

14
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.240
In my humble opinion,
this little swatch isn't enough

15
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.920
to establish gauge for an
overall cable sweater.

16
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.320
I really need to knit a larger swatch.

17
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.320
Now, I won't go on and on and on about

18
00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.720
swatching. I think I've gotten
my point across. On to the shortcut.

19
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.560
The rule of thumb for cables is that they

20
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.640
take 10% to 15% more
stitches than plain knitting.

21
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.520
So let's say we want to run a single cable
up along the edge of our cardigan

22
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.600
or maybe a single cable
down the center of a scarf.

23
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.040
I know you're probably not going
to want to swatch in this case.

24
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.560
So if you've swatched and established

25
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.360
stockinette gauge, just add
a few stitches for the cable.

26
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.040
Again, 10% to 15% more stitches.

27
00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.200
Comparing my two swatches,
both have 20 stitches between the markers.

28
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.880
I'll need to add 6 stitches or so to
my garment to account for these cables.

29
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.560
So if my pattern tells me to cast on 100
stitches, I would cast on 106 stitches.

30
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.480
So the front piece is going
to end up being the right size.

31
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.160
I'll have to block the cable section
heavily for length,

32
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.480
or I might consider a few short rows
to add length to that cable section.

33
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.960
So when adding cables to your knits,
keep in mind that they really impact your

34
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.200
gauge, both stitch and row
gauge and plan accordingly.

